un compilation stages separately, inspect intermediate representations, or integrate with custom workflows."],[276,"The `compile` command performs the following pipeline:"],[1984,"The `flags` field controls execution modes for CIM matrix-vector multiplication. Multiple flags can be combined to optimize computation."],[1920,"The `MEM_CPY` instruction uses optional flags to enable offset addressing for source and/or destination addresses:"],[1530,"The `metadata.op_count` field is optional; when present, it enables TOPS and TOPS/W metrics"],[1531,"The `network_config_file_path` field points to a matrix file defining NoC costs."],[1664,"The `op_count` value comes from the `metadata.op_count` field in the instruction file. This field records the total number of high-level operations (e.g., multiply-accumulate) in the workload, as computed by the compiler."],[831,"The `run` command group provides end-to-end pipeline execution. It handles compilation and simulation in a single invocation, making it the recommended approach for most workflows."],[954,"The `sim` command runs the cycle-accurate simulator on compiled instructions. Use this command when you have pre-compiled instruction files and want to run simulation separately, or when experimenting with different hardware configurations without recompiling."],[1008,"The `tool_paths.json` file defines paths to CIMFlow tools and resources."],[1594,"The `transfer_id_tag` field pairs each sender with its receiver. A mismatch throws a runtime exception."],[766,"The assembly format shows readable mnemonics:"],[302,"The backend runs a series of MLIR optimization passes to lower the DSL program into ISA instructions. The passes handle function inlining, loop unrolling, CIM-specific dialect lowering, register allocation, and final code emission. The output is always `final_code.json` regardless of the internal pass ordering."],[743,"The CG instructions are organized by core and stage:"],[17,"The chip level describes the top-level organization of a multi-core CIM accelerator, including how cores communicate and share resources."],[24,"The CIM unit performs matrix-vector multiplication (MVM) in SRAM arrays. It contains **K** macro groups, each with **T** macros (both are architecture parameters)."],[1152,"The CIMFlow compiler transforms ONNX models into optimized instruction sequences through a two-level pipeline."],[37,"The CIMFlow ISA defines the programming interface for SRAM-based Compute-in-Memory accelerators. It maps neural network operations to hardware through a hierarchical abstraction model, enabling efficient compilation and execution of deep learning workloads."],[738,"The compilation flow consists of three main stages, each producing inspectable intermediate outputs:"],[223,"The compiler accepts the following hardware parameters."],[132,"The compiler can be invoked through `cimflow compile` for standard workflows or the `cim-compiler` native binary for advanced use."],[412,"The Compiler section documents `CIMFlow-Compiler`, the compiler that converts ONNX neural network models into ISA instruction streams for the CIMFlow simulator."],[1386,"The compiler separates workload partitioning from code generation, enabling optimization at both the graph and operator levels."],[1155,"The Computational Graph level handles workload partitioning:"],[579,"The container working directory structure:"],[1450,"The core runtime follows a four-stage pipeline:\nInstruction Fetch (`IF`) → Instruction Decode (`ID`) → Instruction Schedule (`IS`) → Instruction Execute (`IE`).\nDifferent instructions can occupy different stages concurrently."],[1456,"The core stalls when either condition holds:"],[1453,"The decoder and PC update operate on separate phase offsets within each cycle, allowing instruction decode and issue to be pipelined."],[603,"The Docker image includes all dependencies and example models pre-configured."],[481,"The Docker tutorial image includes `tutorial/exploration.json`. For local installation, save the configuration above to a file before running."],[554,"The download is approximately 3GB. Ensure 15GB or more disk space is available after unpacking."],[604,"The download is approximately 3GB. Ensure 15GB or more disk space is available after unpacking."],[296,"The DSL grammar supports:"],[1240,"The first installation takes **20-30 minutes** to build LLVM/MLIR. Subsequent builds are faster with ccache. Do not interrupt the installation during submodule initialization."],[304,"The five pass groups run in sequence:"],[195,"The following commands are not shown in `cim-compiler --help` but are available for specialized use cases: format conversion, instruction inspection, behavioral simulation, and config template generation."],[351,"The following instruction types are supported in CG task entries."],[1460,"The following table lists the conflict dimensions checked during instruction scheduling."],[1452,"The four pipeline stages map to distinct runtime behaviors."],[1390,"The framework provides detailed performance metrics for neural network models on CIM hardware, enabling comparison of models, quantization schemes, and mapping strategies to identify optimal deployment configurations."],[1394,"The full paper is available on arXiv."],[399,"The graph below corresponds to the full `cim-compiler network` path."],[1451,"The graph below corresponds to the per-core runtime path."],[1241,"The installation script performs the following:"],[2033,"The instruction set is designed based on the RISC philosophy,\nwith each fundamental instruction corresponding to one of three basic operations:\n**compute**,\n**communication**, and\n**control flow**."],[1457,"The instruction stream has been fully decoded (`id_finish` is true)"],[1476,"The ISA defines `TAG` as a synchronization point marker and `WAIT` as a pairwise synchronization primitive (see Inter-Core Synchronization for the architectural specification). In the current simulator runtime, these are implemented as follows:"],[756,"The ISA instructions use numeric opcodes:"],[38,"The ISA models CIM hardware at three abstraction levels:"],[763,"The JSON format is machine-readable but verbose. Convert to assembly for easier inspection:"],[275,"The low-level backend is invoked via the `cim-compiler compile` command. It is used directly for DSL programs and indirectly during OP-level kernel generation."],[400,"The Model Simplify stage performs ONNX preprocessing, including operator fusion (e.g., BatchNorm folding) and shape inference. The CG IR output (`instructions_<...>.json`) serves directly as OP-level input — when running the two-step process, the CG output directory becomes the OP input path."],[1392,"The modular architecture supports experimentation with new compilation strategies, enabling development and evaluation of custom optimization passes for workload partitioning, memory management, and instruction scheduling."],[180,"The native `cim-compiler` binary provides additional commands and options not exposed through `cimflow compile`."],[194,"The native `cim-compiler` uses uppercase flags (`-T`, `-K`, `-B`, `-C`) while `cimflow compile` uses lowercase (`-t`, `-k`, `-b`, `-c`)."],[1535,"The network matrix file must exist at the configured path"],[1528,"The number of core entries must equal `core_cnt`"],[1919,"The opcode bits `X` and `Y` control immediate offset addressing for source and destination:"],[1973,"The opcode bits `XX` specify the comparison operation:"],[1160,"The Operator level generates executable instructions:"],[1317,"The output directory contains simulation results including performance metrics."],[643,"The Pipeline Command"],[699,"The pipeline executed three stages:"],[740,"The progress bar shows dynamic programming solving the optimal graph-to-stage mapping. Each \"prefix\" represents a subgraph being optimized."],[1704,"The report file is generated as `simulation_<inst_stem>.txt` under `--output-dir`. Native options like `--format`, `-j`, `--list-cores`, 